 Users can tag models with `^` in the composer. The resulting session-local `m1`, `m2`, … pseudonyms are accepted as `agent` by task, eval `agent()`, and `workpool()`; each uses the bundled task template pinned to the tagged selector. A `^provider/model:level` tag also sets the agent's initial effort, which the `effort` hint does not override. A user-tagged `^provider/model` agent without a level grants its tagged model for per-call selection, subject to `task.perCallModelAllow`; a per-call `model` for a level-tagged agent is a conflict error. See [user-tagged model agents](../task-agent-discovery.md#user-tagged-model-agents) for persistence, boundaries, and precedence.
 
-When per-call selection is enabled for a surface, the task/eval tool descriptions list approved role models from the operator's settings and the narrowing allowlist; project settings never add a model and can only narrow the list.
+When per-call selection is enabled for a surface, the task/eval tool descriptions list approved role models from the operator's settings and the narrowing allowlist; project settings never add a model and can only narrow the list. The task description lists each configured role as `@role` with its model, tells the agent to pass the role when the user names a model or tier (never a guessed version or ID), to omit `model` when the user names none, and to call `ask` with the roles as options when it cannot tell which model the user means.
 
 Runtime stays permissive: the flat form is accepted even while `task.batch` is on (internal callers such as the commit flow's `analyze_files`, and stale transcripts). The model only ever sees one shape. Settled results report the requested and actual model; extension routing, auth fallback, and runtime retry fallback can switch the actual model under the existing policies.
 
